rationale

A Mathematical Theory of Data is concerned with describing, reasoning about and programming data abstractly. Its purpose is to develop a mathematical understanding of data specifications that is independent of particular representations and technologies. It seeks to identify general principles governing the structure and goodness of data specifications, to provide a basis for reasoning about relationships and their representations, and ultimately to support programming that operates on the basis of those abstract structures.

My professional lifetime obsession — describing data abstractly — is actually the foundation of the other two. But the arrival of AI makes the whole programme newly urgent. When generating, transforming, querying and combining data becomes cheap, understanding what the data represents, how its representations relate, and what can legitimately be done with it becomes correspondingly more valuable.